  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1028 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nFor today, the synoptic pattern doesn't change much with the ridge \naxis extending from the Southern Plains. PWs come down a bit (1.1-\n1.2\") from yesterday as the warmer air aloft from the high limits \nstorm coverage in the lowlands. The moisture plume remains out W \nwith scattered storms in the Gila and isolated coverage for the Sacs \nand W of the Divide. Gusty winds to 30 mph and brief downpours are \nthe main threats. The RGV should stay dry today (0-10% chances), \nincluding Las Cruces and El Paso. Outflows off the Gila could spark \na few showers this evening W of the RGV.\n\nThe high elongates across the southern US Tue-Wed due to a passing \nupper trough across the Midwest. Again, forcing will be hard to come \nby on Tue due to the warmer air aloft and general subsidence. Better \ninstability (near 1000 J/kg CAPE) is modeled for tomorrow with \nstronger wind gust potential and area-wide storm chances. The trough \nthat squashes the high midweek slips in from the Plains Wed-Thu and \nbrings some moisture and lift. High pressure aloft centralizes over \nthe Desert SW during that time with storm motion from the north, \nwhich typically means potential strong/severe winds rolling down the \nupper RGV/Tularosa Basin. \n\nThe upper high is pushed E to the Southern Plains/lower MS River \nValley by a trough passing through the northern Rockies for the \nweekend and early next week. The GEFS is less excited about the \ntrough/cold front making it down here from the Plains early next \nweek, while the 6z GFS run has the front reaching us Mon/Tue. Long-\nrange NBM guidance has some cooling by the middle of next week with \na good amount of uncertainty in temps. High pressure aloft to the \neast will get our monsoon moisture flow going again late in the \nperiod. We'll see exactly where the moisture plume and any \nshortwaves set up to determine the favored areas for storms and \nheavy rain potential. PWs next weekend look to be in the 1.1-1.3\" \nrange, which is around the 75th %-ile for mid-Sep.\n\nTemperatures will be slightly above normal (4-8 degrees) through the \nweek with lowland highs in the 90s. Some spots along the lower RGV \nwill approach 100 into the weekend. Depending on the speed and \norientation of the cold front early next week, temps look to fall to \nnear or below normal Mon/Tue. Winds remain light from the SE through \nthe weekend with next week's potential cold front bringing breezy \nE/NE winds.",
